From: evergo <evergo@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH 0088e] evergo: strip CS_HARD/CS_KILL at exec when cs enforcement is disabled

Without a trust cache the pages of Apple-signed binaries never
validate; launchd's CodeDirectory carries CS_KILL, so the first
page-in killed init with SIGKILL (CS_KILLED) even with
cs_enforcement_disable=1.  When both enforcement knobs are off,
clear CS_HARD/CS_KILL on every execed image so invalid pages only
taint the process.
---
diff --git a/bsd/kern/kern_exec.c b/bsd/kern/kern_exec.c
index 68612db..6f14796 100644
--- a/bsd/kern/kern_exec.c
+++ b/bsd/kern/kern_exec.c
@@ -1404,6 +1404,20 @@ grade:
 	if (proc_getcsflags(p) & CS_EXEC_SET_ENFORCEMENT) {
 		imgp->ip_csflags |= CS_ENFORCEMENT;
 	}
+#if defined(ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_SWEET)
+	/*
+	 * evergo (0088e): we run with no trust cache, so pages of Apple-signed
+	 * binaries can never validate; Apple's launchd carries CS_KILL in its
+	 * CodeDirectory, so the first page-in of init killed it with SIGKILL
+	 * (CS_KILLED, namespace 9 code 0xa) even with the
+	 * cs_enforcement_disable boot-arg honored.  When both enforcement knobs
+	 * are off, strip the kill/hard bits from every execed image: invalid
+	 * pages then only taint the process instead of killing it.
+	 */
+	if (!cs_system_enforcement() && !cs_process_enforcement(p)) {
+		imgp->ip_csflags &= ~(CS_HARD | CS_KILL);
+	}
+#endif
 	if (proc_getcsflags(p) & CS_EXEC_INHERIT_SIP) {
 		if (proc_getcsflags(p) & CS_INSTALLER) {
 			imgp->ip_csflags |= CS_INSTALLER;
